  "body": "Found while fixing #630 (PR #640). It is the same class of bug as the nose-centre one.\n\n**Today:** grooving and parting toolpaths post the blade's width-centre as the controlled point. A grooving or parting blade is touched off on a **corner** (LinuxCNC Lathe User Information §4.2), so with normal touch-off a groove lands **width/2 off in Z** and a part-off happens width/2 from where it was asked.\n\n**Expected:** grooving and parting tools carry a reference corner (which side was touched off, as an orientation like #630's), and the shared CL→control-point conversion (`crates/post/src/post/nose_tip.rs`) shifts the blade path by width/2 (plus any corner radius) to that corner. A cut the corner can't reach is refused by name.\n\n**Done means:** a 3 mm groove at Z-10 (centre) posts the touched-off corner at Z-8.5 or Z-11.5 per the reference side; a part-off at Z-20 cuts the part to length; existing groove/part goldens are regenerated with the shift explained.\n\nVerify first: confirm on the engine's groove CL which point it emits (width centre vs a corner) before changing anything. If it already emits a corner, close this as not-a-bug.",
